# curlbench target profile: ppc (32-bit, big-endian).
#
# Toolchain: the distribution cross packages, so a result here is reproducible
# by anyone from a package name rather than from a vendor SDK.
#
#   Debian/Ubuntu:  apt install gcc-11-powerpc-linux-gnu
#   Fedora:         dnf install gcc-powerpc64-linux-gnu   (see note below)
#
# Install the VERSIONED package. The unversioned gcc-powerpc-linux-gnu
# metapackage is uninstallable on Ubuntu 22.04 (it is pinned at 11.2.0 while
# the archive moved the real package to 11.4.0, and apt refuses to bridge
# that); it is also the only thing that provides the unversioned
# powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc symlink, hence the fallback below.
#
# The compiler package pulls in the cross libc development files, including the
# static libc the default STATIC=1 link needs.
#
# Tuned for an e300c3 core: 32-bit, big-endian, hardware FPU, single core, no
# L2. This is the most constrained of the three profiles, so it is where a
# per-byte or per-allocation cost shows up most clearly, and it is the only one
# that exercises big-endian and a 32-bit size_t -- portability bugs the other
# two hide. Run --verify on the target before reading any timing: a case that
# silently computes the wrong thing is not a benchmark result.
#
# KERNEL SKEW, AND IT IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. The distribution cross glibc is
# current (Ubuntu 22.04 ships 2.35) and stamps a minimum kernel into the
# binary. Verified on a binary built with this profile:
#
#   $ file curlbench
#   ELF 32-bit MSB executable, PowerPC ... for GNU/Linux 3.2.0
#   $ readelf -n curlbench   ->   NT_GNU_ABI_TAG: OS: Linux, ABI: 3.2.0
#
# On anything older the binary exits immediately with "FATAL: kernel too old"
# -- it never reaches main(), so there is nothing to measure. Check `uname -r`
# on the target and compare it against that ABI tag BEFORE spending a build.
#
# If the target kernel is older than 3.2, this profile cannot produce a
# runnable binary at all. The fix is a musl cross toolchain (static musl has no
# such floor above 2.6) or a toolchain built against the target's own kernel
# headers. Neither is a distribution package, so that is a real limitation of
# staying on distribution packages rather than something to work around by
# guessing. Note the harness itself is fine: it compiles clean for 32-bit
# big-endian under -Wall -Wextra, so only the libc floor is in the way.
#
# Fedora ships only a powerpc64 cross gcc; for 32-bit big-endian, use the
# Debian/Ubuntu package or add -m32 and a matching multilib.

CB_HOST="powerpc-linux-gnu"
CB_CFLAGS="-O2 -g -mcpu=e300c3"
if [ -z "$CB_CC" ]; then
  if command -v powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    CB_CC="powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc"
  else
    CB_CC="powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc-11"
  fi
fi
CB_NOTE="ppc 32-bit big-endian, e300c3 (single core, no L2)"
